  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 533 AM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper level low centered over eastern OR will continue to sag \nsouthward this morning. Lingering mid and upper level support for \nsnow will continue to wane as this occurs. Most lingering light snow \ndiminishes through the morning, with some light precipitation over \nSW MT south of I-90 this afternoon as a weak embedded upper \ndisturbance pivots in on the northeastern side of the upper low.\n\nSubtle ridging begins to move in late tonight, which will help clear \nskies a bit. Given recent precipitation the concern will be for both \ncold overnight lows as well as for fog by Tuesday morning in many \nareas. This concern more or less exists Wednesday morning as well, \nthough there will be uncertainty with how much snow pack remains by \nthen.\n\nAfter upper troughing moves across AB/SK Wednesday night into \nThursday an upper level ridge begins to move in. Dry conditions \nremain in place, with highs trending back above average by the \nweekend. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nLingering snow this morning:\n\nAs of issuance of this discussion, impactful snow has come to an end \nacross SW MT. Improvements spread further north and northeast through \nthe remainder of the morning, with areas near Havre being the last \nto see the improvements. While snow is coming to an end, impacts \nfrom the snow that has fallen will not immediately follow. Continue \nto plan for additional time to get to your destination today. \n\nFog concerns the next few mornings:\n\nForecast soundings suggest there will be a few mornings of fog \nacross the region early this week. While confidence in dense fog at \nthis time horizon is lower for any specific area, those with travel \nplans the next few mornings should be aware of this potential. \n\nA few soundings feature rather deep saturated layer near the \nsurface. While a drizzle or freezing drizzle is not presently \nforecast (Fog is favored), it will bear monitoring, especially in \nareas where surface temperatures fall below freezing each morning. \n-AM",
